#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092019 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d59733f-3396-5d59-98a9-3f2e0ccfbbc4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Image of an Object Under a Linear Transformation Given a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "3e096041-22f8-5b7e-88d7-eca10203a379" :question/math-academy-id "e-5470" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039564/assets/e8ff769df4089e6ed9a47d56d7317acf41b8aef6a255abc705c0e7ec31a446ea.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $\\mathbf{T}(D)$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\det(T) = 4 \\cdot 2 -  2 \\cdot (-1) = 10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,10\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092020 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51f9b53-0ce5-518f-8173-abe4d87f2851" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Image of an Object Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "e0f036c5-c27d-5a0e-aeb1-cb7c99b49691" :question/math-academy-id "e-5471" :question/problem "A triangle $D$ has vertices $(1,1)$, $(4,5),$ and $(-2,1).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039564/assets/ee8f0cf16983d87fa505303ed6372a3f389518d05b3e13f2e9e2121038792b84.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $\\mathbf{T}(D)$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\det(T) = (-7) \\cdot 3 -  5 \\cdot (-2) = -11.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|-11|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092074 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80a4eee7-a763-5c28-ae3c-5bce78222c87" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Area Scale Factor of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "53c7a748-dadb-516e-879b-92cd0c986041" :question/math-academy-id "e-7454" :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|\\det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =|8-3| \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51f9b53-0ce5-518f-8173-abe4d87f2851" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Image of an Object Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4ae30c6-ac05-575a-b0f4-76a7ab7caef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4ae30c6-ac05-575a-b0f4-76a7ab7caef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303275} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f393b8e-2297-5b65-897e-ea43622fccaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "215b2454-d24f-559c-8109-17676c571bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-275983" :question/problem "The triangle $D$ has vertices $(2,2),(4,6),(6,2).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039564/assets/a4e507191aecd02e16fafba29598972df423e975dd554b260ca355f0cf6476a1.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4=8.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot (-2)=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,6\\,|\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80a4eee7-a763-5c28-ae3c-5bce78222c87" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Area Scale Factor of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e1b40d-5e5a-5e30-89c8-edac91f58529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303281} {:answer/id #uuid "3831a940-666b-51f6-878e-71b88512ceae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16e1b40d-5e5a-5e30-89c8-edac91f58529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303281} :answer-field/id #uuid "182ce720-e152-57eb-9369-c8490a44193b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "182ce155-065c-526e-891c-1c2103b06de4" :question/math-academy-id "q-316569" :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." :question/worked-solution "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|-10\\cdot 8-9\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =|-80-9| \\\\  & =|-89| \\\\  & =89.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d59733f-3396-5d59-98a9-3f2e0ccfbbc4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Image of an Object Under a Linear Transformation Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376b8af6-29bc-55ae-be7a-9659c2559891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303289} {:answer/id #uuid "38f1d0b5-d56f-5080-be06-dd709d489c57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f1d0b5-d56f-5080-be06-dd709d489c57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303290} :answer-field/id #uuid "1965bafe-8660-507f-9547-8ea44caad029" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9258d52-01b3-55d0-afcb-ea66a3cfa2e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-316584" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039564/assets/f38424364e3d4efd8ebc416974163e3eb87b3350c0c6b8ec7f5ae4ea69339824.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,4\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51f9b53-0ce5-518f-8173-abe4d87f2851" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Image of an Object Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46e7464d-7629-5806-aa07-04e5640e39cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284009} {:answer/id #uuid "067bb28f-70ae-5ca2-916c-78f24134099e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "067bb28f-70ae-5ca2-916c-78f24134099e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284010} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c99caa6-f201-5a73-979b-5074f5344bf3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d412caa5-3690-592c-8eec-aa466eedc1b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-316606" :question/problem "The triangle $D$ has vertices $(1,1),(5,1),(3,4).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039564/assets/3e2c56766f201095bee0eea101b877e30910c676c998f37d261fd7cf7aabb837.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-2)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,-6\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
